Hey, Ulli.

Did you ever get anywhere with this?  If not, I just figured out today 
how to enter the Dilog onboard configuration utility from the VAX ">>>" 
prompt.

I needed to do this in order to change the CSR address.  The DQ153 was 
configured to CSR address 760404 (hex 20000104).  I had just added some 
new hardware to the VAX, so I needed to reconfigure it to use CSR 
address 760450 (hex 20000128).

First I booted the VAX (MicroVAX 3900) to the ">>>" prompt.  I did a 
"SHOW QBUS" to determine the current address of the DQ153.

 >>> show qbus
(to get a listing of the Qbus I/O space)

 >>> deposit/p/w 20001f40 20
 >>> deposit/l 20088008 80000002
(to set up the Qbus address maps, or so I have read)

 >>> deposit/p/w 20000106 3fff
(the "20000106" is the current hex address + 2)
(I think "3fff" is the code to access the onboard utility)

 >>> s 200
(to actually run the onboard utility)

This gave me this...
--------------------------------------------------------------
DILOG On Board Configuration Menu     IP/SA Address = 160404
Version: K     Model: DQ153
--------------------------------------------------------------

 From here, I chose menu option "3 - DISPLAY CHARACTERISTICS" to see 
what the current settings were.  To change the CSR address, I chose "4 - 
SET CONTROLLER CHARACTERISTICS".  This let me type in any value I wanted 
for the "IP/SA Address".  Since I wanted to use 760450, I typed in 
"160450" when it asked me for the new value.  To leave all the other 
settings alone, just hit a <CR>.

Once I was finished changing the settings, it asked me if I wanted to 
save the changes.  I told it "y", and it saved the new configuration 
into NOVRAM.  I turned the VAX off and turned it back on.  When I got 
back to the ">>>", I did another SHOW QBUS, and the DQ153 was sitting at 
its new address, 760450, with the interrupt vector automatically 
assigned to it by the system.  Woohoo!


I hope this helps you configure your DQ153, or anybody else who searches 
for days on Google like I did.  I pieced all this together from 
combining instrcutions that I found for the CMD CQD-220TM and the Dilog 
SQ706A SCSI controllers.

If you still need an actual manual, you may try contacting Dilog at 
http://www.dilog.com or http://www.dilog.ch .  I actually telephoned the 
US office, and they said they could send me one for a $50 fee.  I guess 
that this is reasonable, considering that this product is no longer 
officially supported, so they would actually have to pay someone to pull 
the documentation out of archives and reproduce it.


Hope this helps!

--Alex




