On Thu, 4 Jan 2007, Tom Linden wrote:

> The original alphas lacked a sign-extended byte/short integer move
> so that if you want something like c = a + b;  where the variables were
> 16bit integers, took 19 tics IIRC. Had these bit 32 or 64 bit integers
> only 4 tics were required  Considering that they were replacing
> a VAX where a lot of the code, Fortran, Cobol, PL/I had these data types
> in exisiting applications, this was a serious design flaw, and shows lack
> of managment direction.  This was later corrected with 21164 cpu.

Also, operations on data of those sizes (even stores) would be non-atomic 
and would interfere with neighbouring data.

This could result in funny bugs for multitasking code.

> String and BCD support should have been there, even the 8086 had that.

Some of it were even in the 8080 and the Z80.

Simple block moves and block fills would have been enough, wouldn't it?
The VAX string instructions were a tad more complicated than I like ;)

Block operations should be part of the instruction set because only the 
hardware knows about the dynamic status of caches, prefetch buffers, and 
write buffers.  Probably only the hardware even knows about the size of 
the write buffers and width of cache lines.  Hardware can also make 
decisions regarding source and destination alignment faster than software, 
especially for small block operations.

> Replacing VAX with Alpha has got to be one of the more significant blunders
> in the industry.  Had the money that was wasted on the Alpha been put into
> the VAX, I see no reason why it would have similar clock speeds modern x86

They may have had to spend a lot on the fab anyway if they wanted a fast 
VAX -- I've seen a number like 450 M$ for upgrades to the Hudson fab!

I dunno, maybe they should have fabbed chips for others, maybe they should 
have gone fabless.

-Peter


