>figured out what that means yet.

Do you mean "Can not read system ID"?

At the EFI shell, try the various INFO commands.  ("INFO -?" should show
you the syntax.)  If my memory is correct, INFO SYS will show you the
problem.

Several data items (system ID, serial number, etc.) are stored in NVRAM in
two different places.  The firmware wants both copies to match.  Normally
when you swap a FRU like the system board, you are supposed to modify the
NVRAM in the new component to match the rest of the system.

There's an incantation to do this; I'll try to find it in my notes.  I
don't remember if it requires service mode (which you can access) or
manufacturing mode (which you can't).

The stop boot condition you describe is annoying, since it will keep the
system from auto-booting.  VMS doesn't care about it.


