>    Out DECstation 3100 and 2100, and our VAXstation 3100 and VLC all
>    came with the interchangeable keyboards (I thought they were all
>    LK401, but I'm not sure).  We exchanged these with our VT300 and
>    VT400 series keyboards (LK201 and LK401) without issue.
>
>    The only difference I found was somewhere between the keyboard cable
>    and the OS (may have been different controller chips).
>
>    The VT's and VAXen had no trouble with hot swapping keyboards, but
>    the DECstation would accept no keyboard input once you disconnected
>    the one it was booted with until you rebooted (good thing they
>    were all networked).

LK201 and 401 were interchangeable and used the modular plug; only the
physical size and shape were different from a usability point of view.
As Bob noted they both worked in the VT200 - VT400 series terminals.

The LK411 was an LK401 style but had a PS-2 style plug, used with VT510
terminals.  I used an LK411 successfully on an AlphaStation 200 for a
number of years, using both NT Alpha and VMS, and tested it
successfully on an AS600 before I made a 'server' out of it.  I also
tried it briefly on a DS10 system (VMS V7.3-2) and it worked fine.

I also have an LK450, which is the PS-2 PC keyboard made with the DEC
layout and keys (including F13 - F20).  It also worked acceptably on
the AS200 running NT Alpha and VMS; I don't recall ever having to do
the keyboard mode switch that was put in place to make the keyboard
switch between 'pc' and DEC mode.

My PWS is using an LK46x (462 I think) keyboard that came with it; I've
never tried to see if one of the older ones would work.

